          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em graph complex
            is a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} spanned
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of certain labeled
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            encodes the operation of contracting away
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            in a graph.
          %p
            Two similar but different classes of examples are usually referred to by default as just “the graph complex”, going back to hints in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kontsevich92"} Kontsevich 92, P. 11-12
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Kontsevich93"} Kontsevich 93, 5
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^D
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a{:href => "#IdeaGraphComplexModelsForConfigurationSpacesOfPoints"} graph complex model for the real cohomology of the unlabeled ordered configuration space of points
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a{:href => "#IdeaGraphComplexModelForSpacesOfKnots"} graph complex model for the real cohomology of the space of knots
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            In both cases, the graphs are interpreted as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            and the map which identifies these with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+cohomology"} real cohomology
            of either the (unlabeled &amp; ordered)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+knots"} space of knots
            is given by sending a Feynman diagram to its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} n-point function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator+as+differential+form+on+configuration+space+of+points"} regarded as a differential form on the configuration space
            of
            = succeed "-points," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+amplitude"} vacuum amplitude
            depending on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} class
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"} Wilson loop
            encoded by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            – a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+knot+invariant"} Vassiliev knot invariant
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Beware
            that these graph complex models
            %strong differ only in very small technical detail
            as the manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            varies and the choice between models for configuration space of points or spaces of knots is made (see the
            %a{:href => "#OverviewOfDefinitions"} Overview of definitions
            below), and yet these small details
            %strong completely change the cohomological nature
            of the resulting graph complexes; a point not often made manifest when any given author discusses “the graph complex”.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#IdeaGraphComplexModelsForConfigurationSpacesOfPoints Model for ordered configuration spaces of points
          %p
            The graph complex model for unlabeled &amp; ordered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces+of+points"} configuration spaces of points
            was originally sketched in
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich92"} Kontsevich 92 (p. 11-12)
            and worked out in detail in
            %a{:href => "#LambrechtsVolic14"} Lambrechts-Volić 14
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}^D
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            (
            %a{:href => "#CamposWillwacher16"}> Campos-Willwacher 16
            ) possibly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} with boundary
            (
            %a{:href => "#CamposIdrissiLambrechtsWillwacher18"}> Campos-Idrissi-Lambrechts-Willwacher 18
            ).
          %p
            Here we denote this version of the graph complex by “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Graphs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Graphs
            ”, in contrast to “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KnotGraphs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KnotGraphs
            ” for the other model, discussed further
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#IdeaGraphComplexModelForSpacesOfKnots"} below
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AGraphInTheGraphComplexII.jpg", :width => "160"}/
          %p
            This graph complex is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} spanned
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linearly ordered
            internal and external
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            sends any graph to a signed sum of graphs obtained by contracting one of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            with at least one internal vertex.
          %p
            Under ordered disjoint union of edges and
            %em internal
            vertices this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded-commutative+algebra"} differential graded-commutative algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Graphs
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
